insert into blog_posts (slug,title,excerpt,body,tag,published_at) values ('how-we-price-ai-products-transparently','How we show AI running costs before you buy','Every hosted product on AgentForge shows an estimated per-run provider cost. Here is how the estimate is built, and where its limits are.','AI products have two prices: the one you pay once, and the one you pay every time they run. Most marketplaces only show the first. We show both.
insert into blog_posts (slug,title,excerpt,body,tag,published_at) values ('prompt-injection-defences-in-agent-products','Treating documents as data: our prompt-injection defences','Agents read emails, PDFs and web pages written by strangers. We explain the layered defences every AgentForge product uses.','When an agent reads a document, the document can try to give the agent instructions. That is prompt injection, and there is no single fix. We use layers.
insert into blog_posts (slug,title,excerpt,body,tag,published_at) values ('building-your-first-mcp-server','Building your first MCP server: a practical walkthrough','The Model Context Protocol lets AI clients call tools you define. A walkthrough of the structure we use in the MCP Server Developer Kit.','The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol for connecting AI applications to tools and data. An MCP server exposes tools; an MCP client, such as a desktop assistant, discovers and calls them.
insert into blog_posts (slug,title,excerpt,body,tag,published_at) values ('human-approval-in-agent-workflows','Where humans belong in agent workflows','Agents are good at drafting and bad at being accountable. Here is how we decide which steps need a person to approve them.','An agent that drafts a reply is useful. An agent that sends a refund on its own is a liability. The difference is consequence.
